 trying to explain the log  odd score  coming out of these models  but if you're trying to explain a  classification decision like why was  jane denied credit  the top features  can vary  a lot the sharply values for the most  important features can be very very  different  based on whether you're trying to  explain the classification outcome  or you're trying to explain  a love odd score  and you can get  quite incorrect answers  if you're using one to  infer  answers for the next and maybe an  analogy here is useful so if you think  about the us presidential election for  example  and you're trying to answer the question  of which states are the most important  in determining the outcome of the  election  that's a classification question  and  the answer to that question let's say in  the 2020  election was a state like pennsylvania  if you're trying to instead answer the  question of which states were the most  important in determining the total  electoral college votes that a candidate  got  which is a bit like the log odds  question or the regression score  question  then the answer could be is usually  states like california or texas which  are the bigger states they bring more  votes  so now if you're starting to if you  start try using the  answers for the  regression score or total electoral vote  count score to answer the question about  the classification outcomes you can get  very inaccurate answers and that's where  some of the  gaps are in  in the  uh in some existing methods  [Music]  and so um look  what are some of the  different differentiations between  um what you've built and some of the  other so-called  explainability  tools on the market i'm curious  to understand more about sort of your  values  and  the design decisions you made and some  of the trade-offs  that you considered when you were  building  a generalizable solution in this space  right  so we started getting into some of those  topics  i would say that  there are some very substantial  differences in the core explainability  capabilities  we started getting into that a little  bit where when we talk about sharply  value-based explanations  our methods  are more broadly applicable  they can work with different types of  models they can work with different  types of model outputs  and they're more accurate and performant  if we start getting into some of the  other kinds of capabilities let's say  around fairness around stability of  models  some of the key differentiation there is  around  supporting very rich root cause analysis  so  it's it's  fairly common to  report metrics related to fairness  and in the fairness literature there's a  rich set of metrics  that our product supports but so do  a bunch of other  companies in this area  but the metrics  are just the starting point of a  fairness assessment  so for example  if you think about the disparate impact  ratio  uh in a  employment application and it's  measuring what's the proportion of  women who are hired rather relative to  the proportion of men who are hired  and and if that if there's a big gap in  that ratio let's say women are only  being approved or being hired at  80 percent of the rate at which men are  being approved  then the disparate impact ratio will say  that  there's an 80 percent  disparity between the two groups  that's a very useful metric to  signal the start of a fairness  assessment  but just reporting that is not enough  the next step in that workflow is to  do a root cause analysis  that identifies the reason for that  disparity  and  surface that  to human  assessors so for example in the  employment use case  one of the reasons for that could be  that  for this particular kind of job it  required a  college degree  in computer science  and the proportion of women who applied  for this job  that didn't have the college degree was  much higher than  that for men  so if you're able to surface that kind  of reason then a human in the loop can  then go on to make a judgment on whether  the statistical disparity is truly an  indicator of  unfair discrimination  or is it  for a justifiable reason  and it  and the kind of unfairness that's  emerging through this process needs to  be addressed not through  changes in the data or the machine  learning model but needs to be addressed  earlier in the pipeline through  interventions and education and so forth  so that's a powerful  set of capabilities here around  supporting a deeper root cause analysis  which can then inform adjustments to the  model  or when needed  and adjustments to the training data as  well  similarly similarly that kind of root  cause analysis is also supported  in  other parts  other kinds of ai quality attributes  related to why is the model performance  dropping  or  why is  why is  the model suddenly unstable is it  because the world has changed in a  specific way so that  we can surface that reason and then go  on to adjust the model of the  surrounding roots  so  the core explainability technology  support for deep root cause analysis  that supports  for fairness for stability  error analysis that helps with debugging  identifying model weaknesses and  debugging them those are some areas  where  there are distinctive and significant  advantages  over  uh  other other options in this space  and i think at a platform level one of  the big things is the universality  aspect that i touched on earlier  around supporting a rich class of models  and connections to data stores and  being able to work with  uh seamlessly with different deployment  got  platforms  um and and on that point from an  implementation standpoint um where does  true era fit in the modern data stack  that's a great question  you can think of us as  sitting  adjacent  to the training  and to the deployment  pieces of the stack  and then integrating with the with those  pieces  through certain kinds of connectors  so for example if i focus on the  diagnostics product which is  what model developers use during model  development  then  the  installation  and connectivity involves  building pipes to  get access to the models and to the data  sets  that are  uh used to train and test those models  so we support several different  experiences in this process  one is a web-based ui experience  where  after uh  after a model has been developed  there is a fairly easy way to using a  number of different interfaces to  push the models  onto our service along with pointers to  the data  and then the developers can use our ui  experience ui-based product to test and  debug and explain the models  and then there's a related experience  with notebooks where a lot of model  development in data science happens  through  notebooks and jupyter notebooks  and in this other experience they can  just stain their notebooks  and  have  interface with the  with our  service  through a set of api calls and they can  see all the visualizations and so forth  in the notebook itself  so that's a  tighter integration where  their life does not change much they can  just stay in their notebooks and work  with in their usual way  and leverage the capabilities that we  have for explaining and testing and  debugging models  got it  um super helpful  so  so a pum um we're almost out of time but  i just want to ask you  um  before you go  um you've seen a lot in the data  ecosystem in the last  10 15  years through your research and  your work on this ai quality problem  i'm curious like what's what surprised  you most through this journey  that's a great question um  i think one of the things that surprised  me the most is  the  recognition and willingness  in enterprises  to  do the right thing  so  in the sense that  there's a lot of a lot of discussion  these days around trustworthy machine  learning and responsible machine  learning  and some of it is coming because of  regulatory interventions and  and so forth  but what i find as we have engaged with  lots of customers in this space is that  there are a large number of folks data  scientists as well as  folks in leadership positions  who truly care about ensuring that the  machine learning models in the process  of delivering business value  are  mindful of the  societal impact that they have  so questions around explainability and  fairness  and and performance  and characteristics that might end up  denying opportunities or  offering opportunities in ways that can  hurt people long term  those are top of mind things for a  number of folks  i was pleasantly surprised to see that  many people are  receptive and also very  intentional  about  ensuring that  ai technology gets adopted in a way  that's responsible  and it's almost  instead of it being like a defensive  move in the sense that oh if we don't do  it it'll  it'll hurt us in some way through  regulatory intervention or  bad vr  there's there's increasing recognition  that adopting ai responsibly is good for  business  that  in fact  not only will it have a positive impact  on the world  it can actually boost the business value  of the core  yeah that's emerging from these core ai  capabilities  you might be able to reach a broader  section of  the uh  of the population  and include them financial inclusion  you might be able to  get to new segments that you had not  thought of before  and and that's that's that to me is was  the  was both a pleasant surprise and it  gives me a lot of room for optimism  that  we are starting to hit a phase shift  where there will be a bigger set of  adoption of ai at scale  in core areas of lots of different  verticals  and that  it'll be done in a way that  is responsible and it will be done in a  way that it'll generalize well and  perform well  in production  well um as you know i'm a supporter of  the company and your vision and  um it's great to hear your thoughts on  this broader ai quality  issue um i think sometimes  you know we can get lost in the weeds in  simple explainability or model  performance matters and so  it's great to hear  about your broader perspective  on on the ai quality space and  all the implications of that um so thank  you thanks for joining us on i'm excited  to see how the company tracks and i'm  looking forward to great updates from  you in the future  thank you pete it was a pleasure to have  this conversation with you and look  forward to continuing the conversation  this is a  very big area i would say this is one of  the  biggest  uh areas that will  support  large-scale and  positive adoption of  of ai and machine learning  and  so some of these sub areas that you  alluded to like explainability and model  performance and model governance  it's useful to think of them not as  separate areas but under one umbrella  around ai quality and that's the final  thought that i'll leave  you and our audience with  is to think about all of these sub areas  naturally plugged under  the ai quality  umbrella  and  and that will help us  that broader perspective i think will  help us  bring greater clarity and definition to  the field and to show its connection to  business and societal value  i agree  well thanks for thanks for joining us on  the palm  thank you  and uh  you too and thank you to all of our  listeners um who are out there  we are going to take a break for the  rest of the summer  but we'll be back with more amazing  guests and  new episodes of dc thursday in september  so everyone enjoy your summer and  stay safe and be well
 

